{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And he appeared to him</b> — Joseph presented himself to his father. \"And he wept on his neck a good while (עוֹד)\" — the expression is one of intensifying weeping. But Jacob did not fall upon the neck of Joseph his son, nor did he kiss him, and our Sages said that he was reciting the Shema — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 46:29). And I do not know the reason for \"and he appeared to him,\" for it is self-evident that they appeared to each other when he fell on his neck. Furthermore, it is not the way of honor for Joseph to fall upon his father's neck; rather, he should bow to him or kiss his hands, as it is written (Genesis 48:12), \"And Joseph brought them out from between his knees and bowed to his face\" — and this moment would be even more fitting for bowing. Similarly, every instance of עוֹד in Scripture is an addition to the main thing; it is not like many instances, such as \"He does not lay upon a man anymore (עוֹד)\" (Job 34:23), which means He lays upon him according to his sins and no more. And the correct interpretation in my eyes is that the eyes of Israel were already somewhat heavy with old age, and when Joseph came in the chariot of the viceroy with the crown on his face in the manner of the kings of Egypt, he was not recognizable to his father — and even his brothers did not recognize him. Therefore Scripture mentions that when he presented himself to his father and he looked at him and recognized him, his father fell on his neck and wept on him עוֹד — as one who weeps over him continuously until this very day when he had not seen him. And afterward he said, \"Now let me die, after I have seen your face\" — for it is a well-known matter whose tears are more abundant: whether the elderly father who finds his son alive after despair and mourning, or the firstborn son who is reigning. And do not be troubled by the fact that it says \"and Israel said,\" for Scripture will begin speaking from him and then turn back and mention his name again. Thus above (Genesis 41:48–50): \"And he gathered all the food of the seven years... And to Joseph were born two sons\" — and so in many places, always in the Torah and in Scripture."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 46, :notes {0 "In the phrase וירא אליו, Ramban reads the root as נראה (Niphal, 'appeared') rather than ראה (Qal, 'saw'), following his argument that the verse emphasizes Joseph making himself visually recognizable to his father."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:45:55.915938132Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}